<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joanna I of Naples</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1326">1326</a>–<a href="/years/1382">1382</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joanna I was queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and countess of Provence from 1343 to 1382. Her reign was troubled by the murder of her first husband and wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. She sold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> in 1348 and her rights over Prato to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1351. Having adopted Louis I of Anjou as heir, she was deposed and murdered by her cousin Charles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durazzo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joanna-i-of-naples.jpg" alt="Joanna I of Naples" width="269" height="298" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Berthold Werner · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giovanna_I_di_Napoli_-_Niccol%C3%B2_di_Tommaso_(1360_circa).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
